  "summary": "A distinctive geometric painting tradition of the Ndebele people featuring bold patterns and symbolic motifs.",
  "description": "Characterized by sharp geometric patterns in contrasting colors applied to house exteriors, evolving from traditional designs to include contemporary symbols and political statements. This art form represents cultural identity and resistance, particularly during apartheid when Ndebele cultural expression was suppressed.",
  "culturalContext": "The art found in the traditional homestead of the Ndebele people dates back to a thousand years and is evidenced by the rock art found in the Matopos attributed to the Khoi-San. In 2016 the US Ambassador's' fund for Cultural preservation (AFCP) awarded a grant to document the Ndebele traditional art form of hut painting and decoration. This comes as it is important to pass on the skills and knowledge from one generation to the other.\n\nDuring the 18th century, the Ndzundza Ndebele people of South Africa created their tradition and style of house painting. Until the late 1900s, the Ndebele noted warriors and large landowners. In the autumn of 1883, they went to war with the neighboring Boers. The loss of the war brought on a harsh life and horrible punishments for the Ndebele. Through those hard times, expressive symbols were generated by the suffering people expressing their grief. These symbols were the beginning of the African art form.",
